(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-09"] = {"2.1":{"s":"The derivative dy/dx at a point is the slope of the tangent to the curve there. The normal is the line through the same point perpendicular to the tangent, so its slope is the negative reciprocal. With the point and the slope, the point-slope form gives the equations of both lines. For curves given implicitly or by a parameter, find dy/dx first, then put in the point.","f":[{"x":"y - y_1 = (dy/dx)*(x - x_1)","n":"Tangent at (x_1, y_1); dy/dx evaluated at that point.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.66"},{"x":"y - y_1 = -(1/(dy/dx))*(x - x_1)","n":"Normal at (x_1, y_1), when dy/dx is not zero.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.66"}],"w":["If dy/dx = 0 the tangent is horizontal (y = y₁) and the normal is vertical (x = x₁)."]},"2.2":{"s":"For a small change h in x, the change in f(x) is close to h times the derivative. So the value of a function near a point where it is easy to compute can be estimated quickly: f(a + h) ≈ f(a) + h f′(a). Typical uses are square roots, cube roots, powers and trigonometric or logarithmic values near known ones.","f":[{"x":"f(a + h) ~ f(a) + h*f'(a)","n":"Approximate value for small h (the book writes the approximately-equal sign).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.73"}],"w":["Choose a so that f(a) and f′(a) are easy (√64, not √64.1), and keep h small; angles in radians."]},"2.3":{"s":"Rolle's theorem: if f is continuous on [a, b], differentiable on (a, b) and f(a) = f(b), then the tangent is horizontal somewhere in between: f′(c) = 0 for some c in (a, b). Lagrange's mean value theorem drops the condition f(a) = f(b): there is a c where the tangent is parallel to the chord joining the end points. Rolle's theorem is the special case.","f":[{"x":"f'(c) = 0","n":"Rolle's theorem, for some c in (a, b), when f(a) = f(b).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.76"},{"x":"f'(c) = (f(b) - f(a))/(b - a)","n":"Lagrange's mean value theorem, for some c in (a, b).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.78"}],"w":["Check all three conditions first: |x| on [−1, 1] fails Rolle (not differentiable at 0).","The value c must lie strictly inside (a, b)."]},"2.4":{"s":"At a local maximum or minimum of a differentiable function the derivative is zero (a stationary point). First derivative test: if f′ changes from positive to negative, it is a maximum; from negative to positive, a minimum. Second derivative test: at a stationary point, f″ < 0 means maximum and f″ > 0 means minimum; if f″ = 0 the test fails. For the greatest or least value on a closed interval, also check the end points.","f":[{"x":"f'(c) = 0 and f''(c) < 0","n":"Local maximum at x = c (second derivative test).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.85"},{"x":"f'(c) = 0 and f''(c) > 0","n":"Local minimum at x = c; if f''(c) = 0 use the first derivative test.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.85"}],"w":["A local maximum value can be smaller than a local minimum value of the same function.","In word problems, write the quantity in ONE variable using the given condition before differentiating."]},"2.5":{"s":"The derivative measures the rate at which one quantity changes with respect to another. When two quantities depend on time, the chain rule links their rates: for example if the radius of a balloon grows at a known rate, the rate of growth of its volume follows. Velocity is the rate of change of displacement and acceleration the rate of change of velocity.","w":["Differentiate the relation first, then substitute the instant's values; substituting first turns variables into constants.","A decreasing quantity has a NEGATIVE rate."]},"2.6":{"s":"A function is increasing on an interval if larger x gives larger f(x), and decreasing if larger x gives smaller f(x). If f′(x) > 0 throughout an interval, f is increasing there; if f′(x) < 0, decreasing. To find where a function increases or decreases, solve f′(x) = 0 and check the sign of f′ in each interval between these points.","w":["f′ can be zero at isolated points of an increasing function: x³ increases everywhere though f′(0) = 0."]}};
